[0m[2m• [0mThe three files are structurally sound:

  - All contain exactly 417 rows.
  - All identity, text, and hash fields remain unchanged.
  - Every row has valid labels from the locked vocabularies.
  - No annotations are missing.

  They also provide genuinely useful diversity:

  - All four fields agree on 333/417 tweets (79.9%).
  - Field-level unanimous agreement:
      - Target: 373/417
      - Direction: 354/417
      - Horizon: 358/417
      - Confidence: 355/417

  However, “not less accurate” is not yet established. The agents differ materially: Opus is much more conservative about direction and horizon, while
  Qwen marks substantially more tweets usable. Multiple model sources improve robustness, but their consensus is not an independent correctness
  standard.

  Under the frozen protocol, these remain exploratory AI labels and cannot determine PASS/FAIL. The defensible choices are:

  [0m[38;5;12m1. [0mKeep the formal protocol: two humans label all 417 tweets independently, with a third human resolving disagreements. Use the three agent results
     as a separate comparison afterward.

  [0m[38;5;12m2. [0mRegister a new hybrid protocol before human labeling: use the three-agent ensemble as provisional labels, have a human independently label all 417
     without seeing agent answers, and require additional human review for every AI disagreement. This reduces human effort but produces a different,
     weaker evidentiary standard than the current two-human protocol.

  My recommendation is to keep the three AI files sealed, have you complete the blank CSV without seeing their labels, and then compare your
  annotations against each agent and their majority consensus. That will give us the first actual evidence about whether the agent ensemble is accurate
  enough to support a revised annotation design.

